    "content": "{\n  \"files\": [\n    {\n      \"content\": \"#include <iostream>\\n#include <vector>\\n#include <cstdio>\\n#include <algorithm>\\n#include <climits>\\n#ifdef _OPENMP\\n#include <omp.h>\\n#endif\\n\\nint main() {\\n    int n, s;\\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\\n    // Read matrix using scanf for speed (as original)\\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> mat(n, std::vector<int>(n));\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n            scanf(\\\"%d\\\", &mat[i][j]);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Build integral image (prefix sum) of size (n+1)x(n+1)\\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> pref(n + 1, std::vector<long long>(n + 1, 0));\\n\\n    // First pass: row-wise prefix sums\\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n        long long row_sum = 0;\\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n            row_sum += mat[i][j];\\n            pref[i + 1][j + 1] = row_sum;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Second pass: column-wise accumulation to get full 2D prefix sums\\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\\n        long long col_sum = 0;\\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\\n            col_sum += pref[i + 1][j + 1];\\n            pref[i + 1][j + 1] = col_sum;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    // Search all SxS submatrices using integral image\\n    long long mxSum = LLONG_MIN;\\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime)\\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\\n            long long sum = pref[i + s][j + s] - pref[i][j + s] - pref[i + s][j] + pref[i][j];\\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    printf(\\\"%d\\\\n\\\", static_cast<int>(mxSum));\\n    return 0;\\n}\\n\",\n      \"path\": \"src/solution.cpp\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hypothesis\": \"Use integral image with two-pass OpenMP work sharing during construction (row-wise then column-wise prefix sums using static scheduling) and collapse(2) reduction(max) schedule(runtime) on the search phase. This demonstrates OpenMP work sharing via parallel for directives, reductions via max reduction, explicit scheduling control through static/runtime schedules, and thread granularity via collapsed loops.\",\n  \"notes\": \"This implementation replaces the sequential sliding window with a fully parallelized integral image approach. The prefix sum construction is split into two independent passes (row-wise then column-wise), each parallelized with schedule(static) for balanced load. The main search loop uses collapse(2) to combine both dimensions, reduction(max) to compute global maximum, and schedule(runtime) to allow dynamic scheduling via OMP_SCHEDULE environment variable. This demonstrates all four aspects of the goal: work sharing (parallel for), reductions (max), scheduling control (static/runtime), and thread granularity (collapse).\",\n  \"sources_kept\": [],\n  \"transformations\": [\n    \"Replace sliding window algorithm with integral image (prefix sum) for O(1) submatrix queries\",\n    \"Parallelize row-wise prefix sums using #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\",\n    \"Parallelize column-wise accumulation using #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\",\n    \"Use collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime) on the search loop to demonstrate work sharing, reductions, scheduling control and thread granularity\"\n  ]\n}",
    "thinking": "We are role C with goal \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\". The current source is a sequential sliding window algorithm for maximum sum submatrix. We need to propose an OpenMP parallelization that demonstrates these concepts: work sharing (parallel for), reductions (max reduction), scheduling (static/dynamic/runtime), and thread granularity (e.g., collapse). \n\nWe have history of previous experiments, many correct with scores around 0.69-0.70, mean times ~0.059s. The baseline is 0.1346s. So we need to maintain correctness while possibly improving speed further.\n\nThe current sequential algorithm uses a snake-like sliding window: it moves right across columns, then down one row and leftwards, etc., updating sum incrementally in O(1) per step (except when moving down rows which costs 2*s operations). The total complexity is O(N^2 * S?) Actually the sliding window reduces to O(S*N^2) but with incremental updates it's O(N^2) for each submatrix? Let's analyze: There are (N-S+1)^2 submatrices, and each step does 2*s operations when moving down a row. So total ops ~ (N-S+1)*(N-S+1)*something? Actually the snake pattern visits each possible top-left corner exactly once. For each move right/left it updates sum in O(1) per column shift (subtract exiting col, add entering col). When moving down a row, it subtracts entire exiting row and adds entire entering row: that's 2*s operations. So total ops ~ (N-S+1)*(N-S+1)*? Actually number of moves right/left is about (N-S) per row, so total column shifts = (N-S+1)*(N-S). Each shift O(1). Number of down moves = (N-S) times each with 2*s ops. So overall complexity ~ O(N^2 + N*S) which for S up to N is O(N^2). That's good.\n\nBut we need parallelization. The sliding window algorithm has data dependencies: the sum at each step depends on previous sum and row/col updates. It cannot be easily parallelized with simple work sharing across iterations because of sequential dependency. However, we can use a different approach: compute integral image (prefix sums) to allow O(1) submatrix sum queries, then parallelize over all possible top-left corners using collapse(2) reduction(max). That's what many previous experiments did and achieved good speedups (~0.06s vs 0.1346 baseline). The current sequential sliding window might be faster than naive integral image? Actually the baseline is the provided solution.cpp which uses sliding window, but we can replace it with integral image approach.\n\nGiven role C goal: \"OpenMP work sharing, reductions, scheduling and thread granularity\". Using parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max) on all (i,j) top-left corners demonstrates work sharing (parallel for), reduction (max), scheduling (we can specify schedule(runtime) or static). Thread granularity is controlled by collapse which creates larger chunks per iteration.\n\nWe need to ensure correctness: integral image construction must be correct. We'll do two passes: first compute row-wise prefix sums, then column-wise cumulative sum to get 2D prefix sum array of size (n+1)x(n+1) with zero padding for simplicity. Then submatrix sum from (i,j) to (i+S-1, j+S-1) = pref[i+S][j+S] - pref[i][j+S] - pref[i+S][j] + pref[i][j].\n\nWe'll parallelize the prefix construction? Could use OpenMP but careful with dependencies. For simplicity we can do sequential prefix construction; it's O(N^2) and may be fast enough. But to demonstrate work sharing, we could also parallelize prefix sums using two passes: first row-wise parallel for (each row independent), then column-wise parallel for (each column independent). That shows work sharing.\n\nThen the main search loop: #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime) or static. We'll use schedule(runtime) to allow dynamic adjustment, but we need to set OMP_SCHEDULE environment variable? Actually runtime means it uses the value of OMP_SCHEDULE at runtime. But we can also specify static for low overhead.\n\nGiven previous experiments: many used collapse(2) reduction(max) and got correct results with times ~0.06s. The best score was 0.6941 from experiment 6d295b5f520242c0 (mean_seconds=0.0593). That likely already uses integral image approach.\n\nWe need to propose a new candidate that might improve further or at least maintain correctness while demonstrating the concepts. Since we are role C, we should focus on work sharing, reductions, scheduling, thread granularity. We can try using schedule(static) with chunk size maybe? Or use collapse(2) but also consider using tasks for more fine-grained control? But tasks might be overkill.\n\nGiven that previous experiments already achieved good scores, we need to ensure our code is correct and possibly faster. The current sequential sliding window algorithm may not be parallelizable easily; integral image approach is the standard way.\n\nWe'll write a new solution.cpp with:\n- Integral image construction: two passes using OpenMP parallel for (work sharing).\n- Search loop: #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime)\n- Use int64_t for intermediate sums to avoid overflow? The problem says result fits 32-bit signed integer, but prefix sums may exceed that. We'll use long long or int64_t.\n\nWe must preserve the input/output format exactly as original (reading with scanf and printing with printf). Also include necessary headers: <iostream>, <vector>, <cstdio>, <algorithm> for max? Actually we can use std::max.\n\nTransformations:\n- Replace sliding window algorithm with integral image approach.\n- Add OpenMP parallelization for prefix sum construction (row-wise then column-wise).\n- Use collapse(2) reduction(max) schedule(runtime) on search loop.\n- Possibly add #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static) for row/column passes.\n\nHypothesis: \"Use integral image with two-pass OpenMP work sharing during construction and collapse(2) reduction(max) schedule(runtime) during search to demonstrate reductions, scheduling control, and thread granularity.\"\n\nSources_kept must be [] because integration_source_ids is empty.\n\nWe'll output the complete file content.\n\nLet's write code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <algorithm>\n#ifdef _OPENMP\n#include <omp.h>\n#endif\n\nint main() {\n    int n, s;\n    std::cin >> n >> s;\n    // Read matrix using scanf for speed (as original)\n    std::vector<std::vector<int>> mat(n, std::vector<int>(n));\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            scanf(\"%d\", &mat[i][j]);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Build integral image (prefix sum) of size (n+1)x(n+1)\n    std::vector<std::vector<long long>> pref(n + 1, std::vector<long long>(n + 1, 0));\n\n    // First pass: row-wise prefix sums\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n        long long row_sum = 0;\n        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n            row_sum += mat[i][j];\n            pref[i + 1][j + 1] = row_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Second pass: column-wise accumulation to get full 2D prefix sums\n    #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n    for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {\n        long long col_sum = 0;\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n            col_sum += pref[i + 1][j + 1];\n            pref[i + 1][j + 1] = col_sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Search all SxS submatrices using integral image\n    long long mxSum = -9223372036854775807LL; // minimum possible (or use LLONG_MIN)\n    #pragma omp parallel for collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime)\n    for (int i = 0; i <= n - s; ++i) {\n        for (int j = 0; j <= n - s; ++j) {\n            long long sum = pref[i + s][j + s] - pref[i][j + s] - pref[i + s][j] + pref[i][j];\n            if (sum > mxSum) mxSum = sum;\n        }\n    }\n\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", mxSum);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nBut note: The original output uses %d for int. Since result fits in 32-bit signed, we can cast to int or use long long and print with %lld? The problem says \"Print the sum of the SxS submatrix ... It is guaranteed the result will fit a 32-bit signed integer.\" So printing as %d is fine if mxSum is within range. But our mxSum is long long; we can cast to int or use %lld which should be accepted? The judge compares output bytes exactly, so must match format of original: printf(\"%d\\n\",mxSum). We'll keep that but ensure mxSum fits in int.\n\nWe need to include <climits> for LLONG_MIN? Or just initialize with first sum. Better to compute initial sum from (0,0) and then reduce. But reduction requires initialization; we can set mxSum = 0 initially and use max(0,...)? Not correct if all sums negative. So we need a proper minimum value. We'll include <climits> or just initialize with first submatrix sum outside parallel region? That's messy.\n\nAlternative: Use #pragma omp declare reduction(maximum : long long : omp_out = (omp_in > omp_out) ? omp_in : omp_out) initializer(omp_priv = LLONG_MIN). But that requires <climits>. Simpler: we can compute first submatrix sum sequentially and then use reduction with max. Or just set mxSum to a very negative number like -1e18.\n\nWe'll include <climits> for portability.\n\nAlso, the original code uses assert(s<n) etc., but not necessary.\n\nNow check correctness of integral image: pref[i+1][j+1] after two passes should be sum of mat[0..i][0..j]. Our first pass stores row prefix sums in pref[i+1][j+1] = sum_{k=0}^{j} mat[i][k]. Then second pass accumulates column-wise: for each j, we compute col_sum over i and store back. That yields correct 2D prefix.\n\nBut note that the second pass uses pref values from first pass; it's fine because row prefixes are independent per row.\n\nAlternatively, we could do a single parallelized construction using two loops but careful with dependencies. Our approach is safe.\n\nNow about scheduling: We used schedule(static) for prefix passes (good load balance since rows/columns equal). For search loop, we use schedule(runtime) to allow dynamic adjustment via environment variable OMP_SCHEDULE; this demonstrates runtime scheduling control.\n\nThread granularity: collapse(2) creates larger chunks per thread iteration, reducing overhead.\n\nWe must ensure no data races. The reduction on mxSum is safe with OpenMP's built-in max reduction for long long? Actually #pragma omp parallel for reduction(max:mxSum) works only if the variable type supports it; in C++ long long should be fine (OpenMP 2.0+). We'll use that.\n\nPotential issue: The original code uses int sum and mxSum, but we use long long to avoid overflow during prefix sums. At the end we cast to int for output. Since result fits in 32-bit signed, it's safe.\n\nNow produce JSON response with hypothesis, transformations list, sources_kept empty array, files containing path \"src/solution.cpp\" and content as above, notes explaining changes.\n\nTransformations: [\"Replace sliding window algorithm with integral image (prefix sum) for O(1) submatrix queries\", \"Parallelize row-wise prefix sums using #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\", \"Parallelize column-wise accumulation using #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\", \"Use collapse(2) reduction(max:mxSum) schedule(runtime) on the search loop to demonstrate work sharing, reductions, scheduling control and thread granularity\"].\n\nHypothesis: \"Use integral image with two-pass OpenMP work sharing during construction (row-wise then column-wise prefix sums using static scheduling) and collapse(2) reduction(max) schedule(runtime) on the search phase. This demonstrates OpenMP work sharing via parallel for directives, reductions via max reduction, explicit scheduling control through static/runtime schedules, and thread granularity via collapsed loops.\"\n\nNotes: \"This implementation replaces the sequential sliding window with a fully parallelized integral image approach. The prefix sum construction is split into two independe